.agents/.cache/

## Editor/office lock files
~$*

## OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
__pycache__/

# Local planning notes. Public repo, keep GTM material out of it.
PLAN.md
AUDIT_REPLIES.md
LINKEDIN_LEADGEN.md
COMPETITIVE.md
STRIPE_APP_PLAN.md
VALIDATION.md

# Same rule, and this half was missed: the ledger carries real people's names and
# work emails, and none of them agreed to appear on a public GitHub repo.
growth/pipeline.csv
growth/sequences/
growth/sender.txt
# Deny every ledger-shaped file, not just the one we knew about. The 1-19 Aug
# Outlook cohort was reconstructed into a second .csv on 24 Aug 2026 and would
# not have been caught by naming pipeline.csv alone.
growth/*.csv

# Channel research, drafted posts, partner and prospect research. Same reason as
# COMPETITIVE.md: it names real firms and real people, and it reads as a playbook
# for competing with RecoverFlow. Fine as private notes, not fine in a public repo.
#
# Deny by default. Listing these one by one is what let the prospect ledger sit on
# the public internet for a week, and every new note written since has landed
# outside the list. Add to the un-ignore below only after reading the whole file.
growth/*.md
!growth/README.md
!growth/icp.md

# Stripe app build output. Regenerated by `stripe apps upload`.
stripe-app/.build/
stripe-app/node_modules/

# Google service account keys. The Search Console key belongs at ~/.recoverflow/gsc-key.json,
# outside the repo entirely; scripts/fetch_gsc.py reads it from there and never from here.
# These patterns are the second line of defence, not the first. One PII leak and one
# GitGuardian alert is already the running total.
*gsc-key*.json
*service-account*.json
*-key.json
.recoverflow/
